the nodes at 2nd, 3rd and 4th position form a skipblock. Similarly, the nodes at the 6th, 7th and 8th position form another skipblock.

**Start Node**. The first node in any skipblock. 
**End Node**. The last node in any skipblock.
**Middle Node**. Any node in the skipblock which is not a start or end node.

## Iteration

In a boolean skipfield, the skip is only ever of $1$ node. If `S[i] = 1` in the skipfield array, the `i`-th element is skipped. In the below example, during a forward iteration, each time, we increment the index `i`, look at the current node in the skipfield array `S[i]` and if it is `1` instead of `0`, we skip the `i`-th element in the memory block and continue this process until we find an unskipped element.

```{dot}
//| fig-width: 8
//| fig-height: 2.5
//| fig-cap: "Iteration using a boolean skipfield"
digraph {
  bgcolor="transparent"
  graph [margin="0" pad="0.2" rankdir=TB nodesep=0.1 ranksep=0.3]
  edge  [color="#8A8A90"]

  // label nodes — right-aligned, no border
  node [shape=plain fontname="monospace" fontcolor="#2c56fc" width=2.0 fixedsize=true]
  lbl_idx [label="index"]
  lbl_skip [label="Boolean skipfield S"]
  lbl_data [label="Memory Block"]

  // array nodes — each cell same fixed width
  node [shape=record fontname="monospace" color="#8A8A90" fontcolor="#F3EFE6"
        width=0.4 fixedsize=true]
  i0[label="0"] i1[label="1"] i2[label="2"] i3[label="3"]
  i4[label="4"] i5[label="5"] i6[label="6"] i7[label="7"]
  i8[label="8"] i9[label="9"]

  s0[label="0"] s1[label="1"] s2[label="1"] s3[label="1"]
  s4[label="0"] s5[label="1"] s6[label="1"] s7[label="1"]
  s8[label="0"] s9[label="0"]

  d0[label="5"]  d1[label="42"] d2[label="17"] d3[label="28"]
  d4[label="10"] d5[label="6"]  d6[label="3"]  d7[label="4"]
